statement:
Does a qubit quantum error-correcting code with parameters $((7,3,3))_2$
exist, that is, a three-dimensional subspace of seven qubits with minimum
distance at least three?  Let $\mathcal{P}_7:=\{I,X,Y,Z\}^{\otimes7}$, where
$I$ is the single-qubit identity and $X,Y,Z$ are the Pauli matrices, and for
$E\in\mathcal{P}_7$ let $\operatorname{wt}(E)$ be the number of non-identity
tensor factors.  A $((7,3,3))_2$ code is the range of an operator $P$ on
$(\mathbb{C}^{2})^{\otimes7}$ with
\begin{equation}
  P=P^{\dagger}=P^{2},
  \qquad
  \operatorname{Tr}P=3,
  \label{eq:c733-projector}
\end{equation}
satisfying the Knill--Laflamme conditions
\begin{equation}
  PEP=\frac{\operatorname{Tr}(PE)}{3}\,P
  \qquad\text{for every }E\in\mathcal{P}_7\text{ with }
  1\leq\operatorname{wt}(E)\leq2.
  \label{eq:c733-knill-laflamme}
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:c733-knill-laflamme} says that the code detects every
Pauli error of weight at most two and hence, by linearity, exactly corrects
an arbitrary error on any single qubit.  No stabilizer structure,
entanglement assistance, or other side resource is assumed; the codewords
may be arbitrary vectors of $(\mathbb{C}^{2})^{\otimes7}$.  Writing
$K_{\max}(7,3)$ for the largest dimension of a seven-qubit code of minimum
distance at least three, the question is whether
\begin{equation}
  K_{\max}(7,3)\geq3.
  \label{eq:c733-dimension}
\end{equation}
Such a code would encode one logical qutrit.  A complete answer is either
an explicit rank-three projector satisfying Eqs.~\eqref{eq:c733-projector}
and \eqref{eq:c733-knill-laflamme}, or a proof that none exists.

progress:
The Steane code $[[7,1,3]]_2$ is a two-dimensional seven-qubit code of
distance three \sourcecite{ref:c733-steane}{Ste96}, so
\begin{equation}
  K_{\max}(7,3)\geq2.
  \label{eq:c733-steane}
\end{equation}
A $((7,3,3))_2$ code would strictly improve this dimension.

Angl\`es Munn\'e and Huber derive semidefinite-programming bounds on the
largest dimension of an $n$-qubit code of given distance with exact
rational infeasibility certificates.  Their Table~1 nevertheless retains
the gap
\begin{equation}
  2\leq K_{\max}(7,3)\leq3,
  \label{eq:c733-gap}
\end{equation}
so the rigorous upper bound still permits dimension three
\sourcecite{ref:c733-angles-huber}{AMH26}.
